{
  "image_name": "2c826ce07ffa2fbb43b4a15ac8908bb9.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts how *H. pylori* signaling is regulated in gastric cells under two epigenetic states, and how this changes with Epstein–Barr virus (EBV) infection. In both cases, *H. pylori* delivers the bacterial protein CagA into the host cell, where host kinases such as Src and Abl phosphorylate it. The key difference is the status of the phosphatase SHP1.\n\nOn the left, the SHP1 gene is shown as unmethylated and active. SHP1 is produced and appears to remove phosphate groups from CagA, limiting its signaling output. This is associated with reduced oncogenic activity.\n\nOn the right, EBV is present and SHP1 is shown as methylated, implying transcriptional silencing. With SHP1 suppressed, phosphorylated CagA accumulates because Src and Abl still phosphorylate it but there is less counteracting dephosphorylation. The result is increased oncogene activity.\n\nOverall, the image illustrates a cooperative mechanism between EBV and *H. pylori*: EBV-associated methylation of SHP1 seems to disable a host brake on CagA signaling, thereby enhancing the cancer-promoting effects of *H. pylori* in gastric epithelium.",
